Article 3 An applicant who applies to the relevant court for enforcement of an arbitral award made in the Mainland or the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region shall submit the following documents:
(1) a written application for enforcement;
(2) the arbitral award;
(3) the arbitration agreement.

Article 4 The written application for enforcement shall specify the following particulars:
(1) where the applicant is a natural person, the name and address of such person; where the applicant is a legal person or other organization, the name and address of such legal person or other organization and the name of its legal representative;
(2) where the respondent is a natural person, the name and address of such person; where the respondent is a legal person or other organization, the name and address of such legal person or other organization and the name of its legal representative;
(3) if the applicant is a legal person or other organization, a copy of its business registration certificate shall be submitted. If the applicant is a foreign legal person or other organization, the corresponding notarized and authenticated materials shall be submitted;
(4) the grounds for applying for enforcement and the content of the claim, the location of the respondent's property and the status of the property.
The written application for enforcement shall be made in Chinese. If the arbitral award or the arbitration agreement is not in Chinese, the applicant shall submit a certified Chinese translation.

Article 7 Where an arbitral award is sought to be enforced in the Mainland or the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, if the respondent, after receiving notice, provides evidence that any of the following circumstances exists, the relevant court, after examination and verification, may rule to refuse enforcement:
(1) a party to the arbitration agreement was under some incapacity under the law applicable to him; or the said arbitration agreement is null and void under the law to which the parties have subjected it, or, failing any indication on that matter, under the law of the place where the award was made;
(2) the respondent was not given proper notice of the appointment of the arbitrator or was otherwise unable to present his case;
(3) the award deals with a dispute not contemplated by or not falling within the terms of the submission to arbitration, or it contains decisions on matters beyond the scope of the submission to arbitration; provided that, if the decisions on matters submitted to arbitration can be separated from those not so submitted, that part of the award which contains decisions on matters submitted to arbitration shall be enforced;
(4) the composition of the arbitral tribunal or the arbitral procedure was not in accordance with the agreement of the parties, or, failing such agreement, was not in accordance with the law of the place where the arbitration took place;
(5) the award has not yet become binding on the parties, or has been set aside or suspended by a court of the place where the award was made or under the law of that place.
If the relevant court finds that the subject matter of the dispute is not capable of settlement by arbitration under the law of the place where enforcement is sought, it may refuse enforcement of the award.
If the Mainland court finds that enforcement of the arbitral award in the Mainland would violate the social public interest of the Mainland, or if the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region court decides that enforcement of the arbitral award in the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region would violate the public policy of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, it may refuse enforcement of the award.
